@node	0.4, 0.5, 0.3, Chapter 0
@section Product and sum data

A @code{struct} bundles named fields that all exist at once, the way the
book's pairs and records do; it is a @newterm{product type}, one instance
per combination of its fields' values.

@example rust
struct Rectangle @{
    width: f64,
    height: f64,
@}
@end example

An @code{enum} instead names a fixed set of alternatives, called
@newterm{variants}; exactly one variant is active in any given value, and
different variants may carry different data. It is a @newterm{sum type}: the
number of possible values is the sum, not the product, of what each variant
can hold.

@example rust
enum Shape @{
    Circle @{ radius: f64 @},
    Rectangle @{ width: f64, height: f64 @},
@}
@end example

@code{match} takes a value apart by variant, and the compiler checks
@newterm{exhaustiveness}: every variant needs an arm, or the program does not
compile. This is the book's `` `cond` on the type of the data'' made
mechanical, and it is why chapter 2's tagged data (@ref{2.4}, sketched with
this section's constructs) can be represented as a closed enum here even
though the book installs new tags into an open dispatch table at runtime; the
two designs and their tradeoff are the subject of that section's opening
notes.

@example rust
impl Shape @{
    fn area(&self) -> f64 @{
        match *self @{
            Shape::Circle @{ radius @} => std::f64::consts::PI * radius * radius,
            Shape::Rectangle @{ width, height @} => width * height,
        @}
    @}
@}

let circle = Shape::Circle @{ radius: 2.0 @};
let area = circle.area();
println!("@{area:.2@}");
// => 12.57
@end example

Two sum types are so common the standard library predefines them rather than
leaving every program to write its own. @code{Option<T>} is a value that
might be absent: @code{Some(T)} or @code{None}, with no null pointer.
@code{Ok(T)} or @code{Err(E)}, explicit in the type of every function that
can fail. Section @ref{0.1} already used @code{Option} for a checked
multiplication; @ref{0.8} gives @code{Result} its full treatment, including
the @code{?} operator for propagating an @code{Err} without writing the check
by hand.

@example rust
fn first_even(xs: &[i64]) -> Option<i64> @{
    xs.iter().copied().find(|n| n % 2 == 0)
@}

let found = first_even(&[1, 3, 4, 5]);
println!("@{found:?@}");
// => Some(4)

let none = first_even(&[1, 3, 5]);
println!("@{none:?@}");
// => None
@end example

The runnable listing above is @code{examples/sec_0_4_shapes.rs}.

@subheading Recursive data and @code{Box}

An enum variant that holds another value of the same enum type describes a
type of unbounded size (exactly the shape of the book's own lists and
trees), and Rust needs every type to have a size known at compile time.
@code{Box<T>} breaks the cycle: it is an owned pointer to a heap allocation,
one pointer wide regardless of what it points to, so a self-referential
variant can hold a @code{Box} of the enclosing type instead of the type
itself.

@example rust
enum List @{
    Cons(i64, Box<List>),
    Nil,
@}

fn list_sum(list: &List) -> i64 @{
    match list @{
        List::Cons(value, rest) => value + list_sum(rest),
        List::Nil => 0,
    @}
@}

let list = List::Cons(1, Box::new(List::Cons(2, Box::new(List::Cons(3, Box::new(List::Nil))))));
let sum = list_sum(&list);
println!("@{sum@}");
// => 6
@end example

This is the same trick the book relies on for its own pointer-structured
data, made explicit rather than implicit: chapter 2's binary trees
(@ref{2.2.2}) and Huffman trees (@ref{2.3.4}) are both @code{Box}-linked
enums built from exactly this section's two constructs, and chapter 3's
mutable pairs (@ref{3.3}) reach for @code{Rc} (@ref{0.7}) instead of
@code{Box} only because they also need more than one owner.

The runnable listing above is @code{examples/sec_0_4_boxed_list.rs}.
